# Lab 04 – Copy Files with and Without Context

## Objective

The objective of this lab was to compare SELinux contexts when copying files normally and when explicitly preserving the SELinux security context.

## Part 1 – Copy a File Without Preserving Context

### 1. Create the Source File

I created a file under `/tmp`:

    touch /tmp/sef1

I checked its SELinux context:

    ls -Z /tmp/sef1

### 2. Copy the File

I copied the file to `/usr/local`:

    sudo cp /tmp/sef1 /usr/local/

### 3. Compare the Contexts

I compared the SELinux contexts of the source and destination files:

    ls -Z /tmp/sef1 /usr/local/sef1

The destination file received the SELinux context appropriate for its new location rather than automatically retaining the source file's context.

This demonstrates that a normal `cp` operation can result in different SELinux contexts between the source and destination.

A different context on the destination does not mean that the copy failed. It can be the expected behavior because SELinux contexts are associated with filesystem objects and their locations.

